WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.679
Here's the question this lesson answers. How do you reliably choose between a workflow and an

2
00:00:05.679 --> 00:00:11.840
agentic system? The goal isn't to declare one architecture the winner. It's to give you a

3
00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:17.680
repeatable process for making the right call on any task you meet in professional practice,

4
00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:23.280
and for defending that call to your team and your leadership. The most expensive architecture

5
00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:28.799
mistake is reaching for an agent when a prompt chain would have done the job. Incremental

6
00:00:28.799 --> 00:00:34.000
complexity is the discipline that prevents it. You start with the simplest architecture that

7
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:39.200
solves the problem and add agent behavior only where the workflow demonstrably falls short.

8
00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:46.720
When you do need coordination, an orchestrator is the controlling component that decomposes a goal,

9
00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:53.759
delegates subtasks to sub-agents or tools, and synthesizes the final result. And over-engineering

10
00:00:53.759 --> 00:01:00.240
is the trap on the other side. Adding autonomy that costs more tokens adds operational risk

11
00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:05.360
and creates harder-to-audit execution paths without delivering proportional capability.

12
00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:10.800
Knowing when you're over-engineering matters just as much as knowing how to build sophisticated

13
00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:17.360
systems. The first rule of architectural choice is to start with the least complex solution that

14
00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:23.760
solves the problem. A simple prompt chain beats a complex agent for any scoped, well-defined task.

15
00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:30.080
Simplicity reduces cost, reduces latency, and reduces the number of ways things can go wrong

16
00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:36.400
in production. Here's the practical reality. You can always add agent behavior later if the workflow

17
00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:42.319
turns out to be insufficient for edge cases or expanding requirements. But starting with a complex

18
00:01:42.319 --> 00:01:48.559
agentic system and then trying to simplify it is significantly harder. Complexity is much easier

19
00:01:48.559 --> 00:01:54.639
to add than to remove from a running production system. How do you know when agentic complexity

20
00:01:54.639 --> 00:02:00.639
is actually warranted? Watch for three clear signals. First, the goal is open-ended and can't

21
00:02:00.639 --> 00:02:06.800
be fully pre-specified at design time. You can describe what success looks like, but you can't

22
00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:13.839
enumerate every step required to get there. Second, the tool sequence is unpredictable. You don't know

23
00:02:13.839 --> 00:02:20.479
at design time which tools the system will need to call or in what order. Third, the task requires

24
00:02:20.479 --> 00:02:26.960
dynamic replanning mid-execution because earlier steps produced unexpected results that change the

25
00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:33.279
path forward. If none of these three conditions apply to your task, a workflow is almost certainly

26
00:02:33.279 --> 00:02:40.000
the better choice. Let's put reliability and adaptability side by side because this is the

27
00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:46.720
central trade-off in the framework. Workflows give you deterministic steps, testable outputs, localized

28
00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:53.360
failure, predictable token cost, and systems that are straightforward to monitor and maintain over

29
00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:59.440
time. Agents give you dynamic behavior that handles open-ended goals and a higher capability ceiling

30
00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:06.960
for genuinely complex tasks. But agents also mean variable cost, harder to audit execution traces,

31
00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:13.039
and a broader failure surface that's harder to anticipate at design time. The question is never

32
00:03:13.039 --> 00:03:19.360
which is better in the abstract. It's always which set of properties matches what your specific task

33
00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:25.440
actually requires. There are three specific trade-off axes worth evaluating for any

34
00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:32.080
architectural decision you make. Cost versus capability. Agents spend more tokens per task run

35
00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:38.960
so you need to justify that spend with capability you genuinely require right now, not capability

36
00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:45.759
you might eventually want someday. Auditability versus flexibility. Workflows are fully auditable

37
00:03:45.759 --> 00:03:52.880
step by step while agents trade some auditability for runtime flexibility that's sometimes necessary.

38
00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:59.759
Reliability versus adaptability. Workflows fail predictably at specific identifiable steps while

39
00:03:59.759 --> 00:04:05.520
agents can adapt dynamically but introduce variable failure modes that are harder to anticipate and

40
00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:11.520
reproduce in testing. Work through all three axes explicitly before committing to any architecture

41
00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:18.000
for a production system. The right way to introduce agent behavior is incrementally, not all at once.

42
00:04:18.559 --> 00:04:24.320
Build and validate the workflow version of your system first then run it against real inputs and

43
00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:29.839
identify the specific step where the workflow breaks down or fails to handle the required

44
00:04:29.839 --> 00:04:36.959
variation. Maybe the routing logic fails on unusual edge cases. Maybe a synthesis step needs more

45
00:04:36.959 --> 00:04:43.679
flexibility than any fixed prompt can provide. Replace only that specific step with agent

46
00:04:43.679 --> 00:04:49.359
behavior leaving the rest of the workflow intact and auditable. This approach keeps the system as

47
00:04:49.359 --> 00:04:55.519
simple as the task allows and it creates a clear reviewable record of why each architectural

48
00:04:55.519 --> 00:05:02.320
decision was made. Let's walk through three concrete scenarios to make the framework tangible.

49
00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:09.119
In a workflow scenario you're extracting structured data from a fixed format document. Input is

50
00:05:09.119 --> 00:05:15.600
predictable and output schema is known. A prompt chain with validation handles this cleanly and

51
00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:21.679
efficiently. In an agent scenario you're researching and synthesizing a topic from open-ended web

52
00:05:21.679 --> 00:05:28.480
sources. The goal is unbounded and tool sequences are unpredictable at design time. An agent with

53
00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:33.440
planning is the right architectural choice here. In a hybrid scenario you're routing customer

54
00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:40.239
inquiries to specialists and then synthesizing their responses. Fixed routing feeds into a dynamic

55
00:05:40.239 --> 00:05:47.279
synthesis step combining a routing workflow with a focused agent. When you're evaluating a new task

56
00:05:47.279 --> 00:05:52.880
run through four diagnostic questions. Can every step be authored at design time without runtime

57
00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:58.559
judgment? Is the output format known and testable against a clear specification? Does the task

58
00:05:58.559 --> 00:06:03.839
require the system to select tools dynamically based on results it hasn't seen yet? And does a

59
00:06:03.839 --> 00:06:10.399
failure at one step require dynamic recovery rather than a simple fixed fallback? If you answer no to

60
00:06:10.399 --> 00:06:16.160
two or more of those questions, agentic complexity is likely warranted for that task. If you answer

61
00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:21.839
yes to all four you have a workflow. The framework isn't about finding the most powerful architecture

62
00:06:21.839 --> 00:06:27.440
available. It's about finding the right one for the specific problem you're actually solving.

63
00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:33.679
Architecture stops being guesswork the moment complexity has to be earned. The rule is blunt

64
00:06:33.679 --> 00:06:40.160
and straightforward. A workflow has to actually fail before you're allowed to add anything. So

65
00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:45.920
every piece of complexity in the system can point at the failure that it avoided and earned its

66
00:06:45.920 --> 00:06:53.040
place for. Simple first isn't a limitation. It's how you keep the thing debuggable a year from now.

67
00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:56.959
That wraps up this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

